# Deserialization Vulnerability Detection Rules

## R: Risk First

Goal: Identify insecure deserialization vulnerabilities allowing arbitrary code execution
Risk: Remote code execution (RCE), DoS, authentication bypass, privilege escalation
Context: Applications deserializing data from untrusted sources (APIs, message queues, cookies, files)

## Critical Detection Boundaries

- ALWAYS identify ALL deserialization entry points regardless of format
- NEVER assume format-based serialization (JSON/XML) is safe without analyzing library configs
- ALWAYS trace data flow from network endpoints to deserialization sinks
- ALWAYS check for type handling configurations enabling polymorphic deserialization

## Detection Awareness

- Native serialization (Java ObjectInputStream, .NET BinaryFormatter, Python pickle) is ALWAYS vulnerable
- JSON/XML libraries become vulnerable when type information is preserved
- Protocol-specific endpoints (RMI/1099, JMX, T3/7001) often expose deserialization

## Vulnerability Patterns by Language

### Java
ObjectInputStream.readObject, XMLDecoder.readObject, Kryo.readClassAndObject,
xstream.fromXML, JSON.parseObject, mapper.readValue (with enableDefaultTyping)

### Python
pickle.loads, pickle.load, yaml.load (without SafeLoader), jsonpickle.decode, dill.loads

### JavaScript/Node.js
nodeSerialize.unserialize, serialize.unserialize, eval, Function constructor

### C#/.NET
BinaryFormatter.Deserialize, SoapFormatter.Deserialize,
JsonConvert.DeserializeObject (with TypeNameHandling)

### PHP
unserialize (with user input)

## Binary Format Indicators
- Java Serialization: hex AC ED 00 05, base64 rO0
- .NET Binary: base64 AAEAAAD/////
- Python Pickle: base64 gASV (protocol 4)
- PHP Serialization: starts with a: (array), O: (object)

## JSON Type Handling Indicators
- Jackson/Java: @class, @type fields
- Json.NET/C#: $type field
- Fastjson: @type field

## Severity Assessment

CRITICAL: Native serialization with untrusted input, exposed RMI/JMX/T3 endpoints, type-enabled JSON/XML processing
HIGH: Deserialization behind authentication, custom serialization with unsafe readObject
MEDIUM: Deserialization with class filtering, internal-only endpoints
LOW: Heavily restricted allowlists, no known gadgets available

## Remediation Priority

1. Disable native serialization entirely where possible
2. Remove type handling from JSON/XML parsers
3. Block vulnerable protocol ports (RMI, JMX, T3)
4. Implement strict class filtering if serialization required
5. Use data-only formats (JSON without types) for APIs
